## 4. Apply OOP to our Employee Management project (10 minutes)

Now let's create an employee class.

Open `Program.cs` and replace its content with:

```
Employee employee1 = new Employee();

employee1.name = "Rahul";
employee1.employeeId = 101;
employee1.salary = 35000;

employee1.DisplayDetails();

class Employee
{
    public string name = "";
    public int employeeId;
    public decimal salary;

    public void DisplayDetails()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Employee Details");
        Console.WriteLine($"Name: {name}");
        Console.WriteLine($"ID: {employeeId}");
        Console.WriteLine($"Salary: {salary}");
    }
}
```

Run:

```
dotnet run
```

Expected output:

```
Employee Details
Name: Rahul
ID: 101
Salary: 35000
```

### Understand this code

- `class Employee` – creates an Employee blueprint.
- `employee1` – creates an employee object.
- `new Employee()` – creates a new instance.
- `name`, `employeeId`, `salary` – properties represented here by public fields.
- `DisplayDetails()` – method that displays employee information.

Important: We used public fields for simplicity. Later, we will learn properties, constructors and encapsulation, which are preferred for proper application design.

